{
  "subtype": "causal_attribution",
  "outcome_variable": "non_recommendation_outcome (Recommended = no)",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "rating_consistency",
      "causal_role": "other",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "A descriptive summary of dispersion across the existing sub-ratings (High/Moderate/Consistent variance). It is neither a distinct candidate cause of non-recommendation nor a common cause of any treatment and the outcome; it is a derived meta-feature of the same ratings that largely determine recommendation. Not a confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "value_perception",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "A candidate cause of the outcome: poor/premium-price-complaint value plausibly drives a non-recommendation. Derived from Value For Money plus review text. This is one of the treatments whose effect is being attributed, not a confounder (it is not shown to cause any other treatment)."
    },
    {
      "name": "service_gap",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Identifies which service dimension was weak (food/seat/IFE/staff/multiple). This is a direct candidate cause of non-recommendation surfaced from the sub-ratings. It functions as a treatment, not a confounder or mediator of another treatment."
    },
    {
      "name": "experience_score",
      "causal_role": "mediator",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "A numeric composite/average of the sub-ratings, i.e. an aggregate proxy for overall satisfaction sitting on the causal path between the specific experience factors and the recommendation decision. Adjusting for it would block the very effects being attributed; it is a mediator/proxy, not a confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "issue_type",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Categorises the primary complaint (operational/ground/compensation/product-quality) extracted from review text. A candidate cause of non-recommendation, hence a treatment. It is not a common cause of another treatment and the outcome, so not a confounder."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["value_perception", "service_gap", "issue_type"],
  "confounders": [],
  "confounder_quality": 0.0,
  "rationale": "The operator supplied a rich set of CANDIDATE CAUSES (value_perception, service_gap, issue_type) plus an aggregate mediator (experience_score) and a descriptive dispersion feature (rating_consistency). So the 'treatment' half of a causal setup is present and even over-supplied. However, the decisive requirement for causal ATTRIBUTION is missing: there are NO confounders — no added variable is a common cause of BOTH a treatment and the non-recommendation outcome. Genuine confounders for this question (e.g., type of traveller, route/market, month/season flown, verified status, prior loyalty/expectation level, cabin/aircraft variant) were NOT surfaced as new columns; several such candidates already sit in the original table and none were adjusted-for or elevated. Because the columns are almost entirely treatments and mediators/proxies of the outcome, they cannot support an unbiased effect estimate — attributing non-recommendation to value_perception or service_gap without confounder control would be biased. The capability under test (surfacing confounders from the data/text rather than copying them from the query) is therefore NOT demonstrated, and confounder_quality is 0."
}

